\title{Concavity Certificates for Principal Tropical Characteristic Coefficients}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study coefficient sequences obtained by maximizing integer-valued principal weights over subsets of fixed cardinality, motivated by tropical permanents of principal submatrices in max-plus spectral theory. The matrix-specific hypothesis is a symmetric two-set exchange inequality: whenever principal index sets $S$ and $T$ have cardinalities differing by two, there are two intermediate-cardinality sets $U,V$ such that $w(S)+w(T)\leq w(U)+w(V)$. We prove that this hypothesis forces discrete concavity of the layer maxima $c_k$, namely $2c_k\geq c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}$. Midpoint concavity is equivalent to antitonicity of consecutive slopes and yields the global inequality $c_{j+1}-c_j\leq c_i-c_{i-1}$ whenever $1\leq i\leq j<n$. The hierarchy is invariant under affine changes $c_k\mapsto c_k+ak+b$. Conversely, one failed midpoint inequality obstructs every realization with the exchange property. We give strict quadratic and nonrealizable spike examples, recognition algorithms, applications, and a precise account of the boundary between the abstract exchange theorem and its intended symmetric-matrix application.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
In max-plus algebra the operations are
\[
x\oplus y=\max(x,y),\qquad x\odot y=x+y.
\]
Matrix expressions then encode optimization over weighted combinatorial structures. The tropical permanent of a square matrix is the maximum weight of a permutation, equivalently a directed cycle cover. Principal tropical permanents attach such a value to every principal index set.

For an $n\times n$ matrix $A=(a_{ij})$ and $S\subseteq[n]$, define
\[
\tper(A[S])=\max_{\sigma\in\Sym(S)}\sum_{i\in S}a_{i,\sigma(i)}.
\]
For every cardinality $k$, define the leading principal coefficient
\[
c_k=\max_{S\subseteq[n],\ |S|=k}\tper(A[S]).
\]
Depending on the convention used for the tropical characteristic polynomial, $c_k$ may be called the coefficient of complementary degree. The order convention is immaterial here; we study the cardinality-indexed sequence $(c_k)_{k=0}^n$.

The central issue is exchange between principal sets rather than polynomial expansion. We separate coefficient geometry from matrix combinatorics by considering arbitrary integer weights on subsets, with a two-set exchange inequality between layers whose cardinalities differ by two. This yields local concavity, global slope order, affine invariance, and an explicit obstruction. These conclusions are conditional recognition results: the exchange property remains an explicit hypothesis and is not asserted here for every symmetric tropical matrix.

\section{Definitions and background}
The elementary max-plus translation identity is
\[
a+\max(x,y)=\max(a+x,a+y).
\]
Thus translating candidate scores translates their maximum. This accounts for the affine behavior of cardinality-indexed coefficients under uniform changes of weights.

\begin{definition}[Tropical permanent]
For a real square matrix $B=(b_{ij})$ indexed by a finite set $S$, its tropical permanent is
\[
\tper(B)=\max_{\sigma:S\to S\text{ bijective}}\sum_{i\in S}b_{i,\sigma(i)}.
\]
\end{definition}
A permutation decomposes into directed cycles. For symmetric matrices, reversing an edge preserves its weight. This makes cycle reversal a plausible source of exchange, although the results below use only the resulting inequality.

\begin{definition}[Principal coefficient system]
Let $E$ be finite with $|E|=n$. A principal coefficient system consists of a weight $w:2^E\to\mathbb Z$ and integers $c_0,\ldots,c_n$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $w(S)\leq c_{|S|}$ for every $S\subseteq E$;
\item for every $0\leq k\leq n$, there is $S$ with $|S|=k$ and $w(S)=c_k$;
\item whenever $|T|=|S|+2$, there are $U,V\subseteq E$ satisfying
\[
|U|=|V|=|S|+1,
\qquad
w(S)+w(T)\leq w(U)+w(V).
\]
\end{enumerate}
Condition (iii) is the two-set principal exchange property.
\end{definition}
The first two conditions say precisely that $c_k$ is the maximum of $w$ on the $k$th cardinality layer. For a tropical matrix one takes $w(S)=\tper(A[S])$.

\begin{definition}[Discrete concavity]
A finite integer sequence $(c_k)_{k=0}^n$ is discretely concave at $1\leq k<n$ if
\[
2c_k\geq c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}.
\]
It is discretely concave if this holds at every interior index. Its consecutive slopes are
\[
d_k=c_k-c_{k-1}\qquad(1\leq k\leq n).
\]
\end{definition}

\section{Exchange forces coefficient concavity}
\begin{theorem}[Principal Exchange Implies Coefficient Concavity]
Let $(w,c)$ be a principal coefficient system on an $n$-element set. Then for every $1\leq k<n$,
\[
2c_k\geq c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose attained maximizers $S,T$ with
\[
|S|=k-1,\quad w(S)=c_{k-1},\qquad
|T|=k+1,\quad w(T)=c_{k+1}.
\]
Exchange supplies $U,V$ of cardinality $k$ such that
\[
w(S)+w(T)\leq w(U)+w(V).
\]
Layer maximality gives $w(U),w(V)\leq c_k$. Hence
\[
c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}=w(S)+w(T)
\leq w(U)+w(V)\leq2c_k.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The proof uses no positivity, inclusion relation between $S$ and $T$, or matrix entries. It needs only attained layer maxima and exchange across a gap of two.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Conditional symmetric-matrix consequence]
Let $A$ be a finite symmetric max-plus matrix. If its principal tropical-permanent weight function satisfies two-set principal exchange, then its cardinality-layer maxima form a discretely concave sequence.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Set $w(S)=\tper(A[S])$. Finiteness gives attainment and the assumed exchange property supplies the remaining hypothesis of the theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{Slope order and Newton geometry}
\begin{proposition}[Midpoint--Slope Equivalence]
For every integer sequence and $k\geq1$, discrete concavity at $k$ is equivalent to
\[
c_{k+1}-c_k\leq c_k-c_{k-1}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
This follows by rearranging $2c_k\geq c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global Slope Ordering]
If $(c_k)_{k=0}^n$ is discretely concave and $1\leq i\leq j<n$, then
\[
c_{j+1}-c_j\leq c_i-c_{i-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding proposition gives
\[
d_i\geq d_{i+1}\geq\cdots\geq d_{j+1}.
\]
Transitivity yields the claim.
\end{proof}

For a tropical polynomial
\[
p(x)=\max_k(c_k+kx),
\]
the graph is an upper envelope of affine functions. Adjacent terms $k-1$ and $k$ meet at $x=c_{k-1}-c_k=-d_k$. Ordered slopes therefore order the candidate adjacent breakpoints. A complete assertion that all terms occur on the envelope may require essentiality assumptions, but the coefficient theorem supplies the underlying Newton-diagram order.

\section{Affine invariance}
\begin{theorem}[Affine Invariance]
Let $(c_k)_{k=0}^n$ be discretely concave, let $a,b\in\mathbb Z$, and put
\[
c'_k=c_k+ak+b.
\]
Then $(c'_k)$ is discretely concave, with exactly the same midpoint deficits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct calculation gives
\begin{align*}
2c'_k-c'_{k-1}-c'_{k+1}
&=2(c_k+ak+b)\\
&\quad-[c_{k-1}+a(k-1)+b]\\
&\quad-[c_{k+1}+a(k+1)+b]\\
&=2c_k-c_{k-1}-c_{k+1}.
\end{align*}
Thus all affine contributions cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform slope shift]
Under the affine transformation, $d'_k=d_k+a$. Consequently every pairwise slope-order inequality is preserved.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Subtract $c'_{k-1}$ from $c'_k$.
\end{proof}
The constant $b$ translates the coefficient graph and $ak$ tilts it. Neither changes discrete curvature, so one may normalize a baseline or a selected slope without altering concavity.

\section{Obstructions and examples}
\begin{theorem}[Single Midpoint Obstruction]
If an integer sequence has an interior index $k$ such that
\[
2c_k<c_{k-1}+c_{k+1},
\]
then it is not the coefficient sequence of any principal coefficient system satisfying two-set exchange.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Any such realization would satisfy the opposite weak inequality by the exchange-to-concavity theorem.
\end{proof}
Passing every test is necessary but is not claimed sufficient for realization by a symmetric tropical matrix.

\begin{example}[Strict quadratic profile]
For $c_k=-k^2$,
\[
2c_k-c_{k-1}-c_{k+1}=2,
\]
so every interior inequality is strict. The slopes are $d_k=1-2k$, namely $-1,-3,-5,\ldots$, and strictly decrease.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Spike obstruction]
For
\[
(c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4)=(0,0,10,0,0),
\]
the inequality at $k=1$ fails because $2c_1=0<10=c_0+c_2$. Thus no principal coefficient system with exchange realizes this sequence. A high peak is not itself forbidden; the abrupt approach to it is.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Affine quadratic family]
For $q_k=-k^2+ak+b$, every midpoint deficit remains $2$, while the slopes become $1-2k+a$.
\end{example}

\section{Further structural consequences}
Define the curvature deficits
\[
\Delta_k=2c_k-c_{k-1}-c_{k+1}=d_k-d_{k+1}.
\]
Telescoping gives, whenever $1\leq i\leq j<n$,
\[
d_i-d_{j+1}=\sum_{k=i}^{j}\Delta_k.
\]
Thus the global difference between an early and later slope is the accumulated curvature between them. Vanishing deficits characterize affine portions; positive deficits produce strict slope decrease.

Concavity also orders separated secants. If $0\leq p<q\leq r<s\leq n$, every slope in the later interval is no larger than every slope in the earlier interval, so
\[
\frac{c_s-c_r}{s-r}\leq\frac{c_q-c_p}{q-p}.
\]
Furthermore, the sequence lies above every chord: for $0\leq r<k<s\leq n$,
\[
(s-r)c_k\geq(s-k)c_r+(k-r)c_s.
\]
To see this, compare the average of the first $k-r$ slopes after $r$ with the average of all $s-r$ slopes through $s$. Since slopes decrease, the earlier average is at least the total average; clearing denominators proves the inequality. Hence local nonnegative deficits encode all one-dimensional chord inequalities.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Midpoint-deficit certificate}
Given $c_0,\ldots,c_m$, compute
\[
\Delta_k=2c_k-c_{k-1}-c_{k+1}\qquad(1\leq k<m).
\]
The sequence passes exactly when every deficit is nonnegative. A structured version is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Initialize empty deficit and violation lists.
\item For $k=1,\ldots,m-1$, compute $\Delta_k$.
\item Record $k$ whenever $\Delta_k<0$.
\item Return the deficits and violations.
\end{enumerate}
This takes $O(m)$ time and $O(m)$ reporting space, or $O(1)$ auxiliary space when only acceptance and the first violation are needed.

\subsection{Slope audit}
Compute $d_k=c_k-c_{k-1}$ and verify $d_{k+1}\leq d_k$. This is also $O(m)$ time and, by midpoint--slope equivalence, accepts exactly the same sequences.

\subsection{Affine-invariance audit}
Form $c'_k=c_k+ak+b$, compute the old and new deficit lists, and compare them. Equality is guaranteed by affine invariance. This reporting procedure takes $O(m)$ time and space.

\subsection{Exact principal-permanent exploration}
For a small matrix, enumerate every $k$-subset and every permutation of that subset, maximize each selected-entry sum, and maximize again over the layer. The number of evaluated permutation terms is
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom nk k!=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\frac{n!}{(n-k)!},
\]
which is $O(n!)$ up to a constant factor. Exhaustion is suitable for examples and conjecture testing rather than large instances.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Candidate screening.}
For coefficients proposed to arise from a model expected to satisfy exchange, a linear scan locates every impossible midpoint. A violation remains an obstruction under all affine normalizations.

\paragraph{Diminishing returns.}
The slope $d_k$ is the marginal gain from allowing one more index. Its antitonicity is a diminishing-returns principle and a one-dimensional shadow of richer discrete exchange geometries.

\paragraph{Tropical spectral geometry.}
Coefficient differences control intersections of adjacent affine terms $c_k+kx$. Their order constrains candidate breakpoints, while strict inequalities rule out equal adjacent candidates.

\paragraph{Model diagnosis.}
A negative deficit can indicate absent symmetry, failed cycle-cover exchange, or incorrectly proposed principal-permanent data. The deficit profile localizes the problematic cardinalities.

\section{Scope and limitations}
The principal result is an exchange theorem about coefficient systems, not an unconditional assertion that every symmetric tropical matrix satisfies the exchange axiom. Symmetry motivates alternating-cycle surgery because reversal preserves weights, but constructing intermediate covers in full generality remains separate work.

Discrete concavity is necessary for exchange realizability but has not been proved sufficient for realization by a symmetric matrix. Endpoints are omitted because their two neighboring coefficients do not exist. The integer codomain is convenient, although the arguments use only ordered additive arithmetic and should extend to suitable ordered abelian groups. Finally, reversing a convention for polynomial degree reverses the displayed coefficient order but does not change the underlying three-term inequalities.

\section{Future work}
Five directions emerge. First, prove cycle exchange for symmetric tropical permanents using alternating cycles and reversal. Second, determine whether every normalized integral concave vector can be realized, perhaps using diagonal and paired off-diagonal blocks to encode slope drops. Third, seek a valuated even delta-matroid interpretation of variable-cardinality exchange. Fourth, connect strict midpoint inequalities to distinct, perturbation-stable Newton breakpoints. Fifth, classify low-dimensional nonsymmetric failures through directed cycle covers that cannot be paired with their reversals.

\section{Conclusion}
Attained maxima on layers $k-1$ and $k+1$ exchange into two candidates on layer $k$, forcing
\[
2c_k\geq c_{k-1}+c_{k+1}.
\]
This local inequality is exactly the decrease of consecutive slopes, and transitivity orders slopes across arbitrary gaps. Affine changes preserve every curvature deficit, while one negative deficit rules out every realization possessing the exchange mechanism.

The hierarchy supplies transparent recognition algorithms, examples, and a precise interface between tropical matrix combinatorics and discrete convex analysis. Its principal conceptual contribution is to isolate the matrix-specific challenge: once principal cycle-cover exchange is established for a class of symmetric tropical matrices, the complete concavity and slope theory follows.

\end{document}

